1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,765
Sweetheart, my mother could wrap a present

2
00:00:02,765 --> 00:00:05,266
so beautifully that you didn't want to

3
00:00:05,266 --> 00:00:05,793
open it.

4
00:00:05,973 --> 00:00:08,491
Plain brown paper from the butcher,

5
00:00:08,491 --> 00:00:09,427
a red ribbon,

6
00:00:09,427 --> 00:00:11,441
and a sprig of pine from the

7
00:00:11,441 --> 00:00:11,801
yard.

8
00:00:11,981 --> 00:00:13,049
That's all.

9
00:00:13,229 --> 00:00:15,569
And under our little tree on Elm

10
00:00:15,569 --> 00:00:18,713
Street, her presents looked better than the

11
00:00:18,713 --> 00:00:21,564
ones from the fancy department store in

12
00:00:21,564 --> 00:00:24,708
Cleveland, with their shiny paper and their

13
00:00:24,708 --> 00:00:25,513
store bows.

14
00:00:25,862 --> 00:00:28,679
So tonight I'm giving you seven gift

15
00:00:28,679 --> 00:00:32,435
wrapping mistakes that make presents look cheap,

16
00:00:32,435 --> 00:00:35,018
and my mother's old tricks to fix

17
00:00:35,018 --> 00:00:37,287
every one, most of them free.

18
00:00:37,467 --> 00:00:38,591
And stay with me,

19
00:00:38,591 --> 00:00:40,773
because the last trick is the one

20
00:00:40,773 --> 00:00:43,153
that makes people save the wrapping,

21
00:00:43,153 --> 00:00:45,401
and it takes about ten seconds per

22
00:00:45,401 --> 00:00:45,930
present.

23
00:00:46,280 --> 00:00:48,963
Every year, the week before Christmas,

24
00:00:48,963 --> 00:00:51,857
my mother cleared the kitchen table after

25
00:00:51,857 --> 00:00:54,539
supper and turned it into her wrapping

26
00:00:54,539 --> 00:00:55,104
station.

27
00:00:55,284 --> 00:00:58,065
Scissors, a roll of brown paper,

28
00:00:58,065 --> 00:00:59,976
a spool of red ribbon,

29
00:00:59,976 --> 00:01:01,280
a jar of paste,

30
00:01:01,280 --> 00:01:03,539
and a pile of pine sprigs.

31
00:01:03,719 --> 00:01:05,854
I was allowed to hold my finger

32
00:01:05,854 --> 00:01:06,749
on the knots.

33
00:01:06,929 --> 00:01:09,449
That was the most important job in

34
00:01:09,449 --> 00:01:10,931
the house, she said,

35
00:01:10,931 --> 00:01:12,339
and I believed her.

36
00:01:12,689 --> 00:01:15,460
She never bought wrapping paper in her

37
00:01:15,460 --> 00:01:16,335
life, honey.

38
00:01:16,515 --> 00:01:18,036
She saved everything.

39
00:01:18,216 --> 00:01:20,616
Brown paper from the butcher,

40
00:01:20,616 --> 00:01:22,520
the Sunday funny pages,

41
00:01:22,520 --> 00:01:25,914
old maps, even wallpaper samples from the

42
00:01:25,914 --> 00:01:27,156
hardware store.

43
00:01:27,336 --> 00:01:29,992
And she ironed the ribbon from last

44
00:01:29,992 --> 00:01:31,662
year with a warm iron,

45
00:01:31,662 --> 00:01:33,408
so it looked brand new.

46
00:01:33,588 --> 00:01:35,528
In a minute I'll show you why

47
00:01:35,528 --> 00:01:38,339
plain paper looks richer than the fanciest

48
00:01:38,339 --> 00:01:39,544
roll at the store.

49
00:01:39,893 --> 00:01:41,681
Mistake number one.

50
00:01:41,861 --> 00:01:43,835
Using too much paper.

51
00:01:44,015 --> 00:01:46,057
People wrap a small box in a

52
00:01:46,057 --> 00:01:48,537
huge sheet, and then they fold and

53
00:01:48,537 --> 00:01:51,381
fold and tape over all those bunched-up

54
00:01:51,381 --> 00:01:53,861
corners, and it looks like a lumpy

55
00:01:53,861 --> 00:01:54,371
pillow.

56
00:01:54,551 --> 00:01:55,689
Here's the rule.

57
00:01:55,869 --> 00:01:58,330
The paper should go around the box

58
00:01:58,330 --> 00:02:00,646
with only two inches of overlap,

59
00:02:00,646 --> 00:02:01,805
and on the ends,

60
00:02:01,805 --> 00:02:04,121
only a little more than half the

61
00:02:04,121 --> 00:02:05,424
height of the box.

62
00:02:05,773 --> 00:02:06,918
So measure it,

63
00:02:06,918 --> 00:02:07,817
sweetheart.

64
00:02:07,997 --> 00:02:10,633
Roll the box over the paper once,

65
00:02:10,633 --> 00:02:12,150
all the way around,

66
00:02:12,150 --> 00:02:13,349
add two inches,

67
00:02:13,349 --> 00:02:13,988
and cut.

68
00:02:14,168 --> 00:02:16,559
Then stand the box on its end.

69
00:02:16,739 --> 00:02:19,098
The paper on each side should reach

70
00:02:19,098 --> 00:02:20,512
just past the middle.

71
00:02:20,692 --> 00:02:22,353
Cut off the extra.

72
00:02:22,533 --> 00:02:23,795
With less paper,

73
00:02:23,795 --> 00:02:26,399
your corners fold flat and sharp,

74
00:02:26,399 --> 00:02:27,978
like a hospital bed.

75
00:02:28,158 --> 00:02:30,851
My mother learned that in nurse's training.

76
00:02:31,201 --> 00:02:32,908
Mistake number two.

77
00:02:33,088 --> 00:02:34,400
Tape everywhere.

78
00:02:34,580 --> 00:02:37,255
Long strips of shiny tape all over

79
00:02:37,255 --> 00:02:39,458
the present, on the corners,

80
00:02:39,458 --> 00:02:40,481
on the sides,

81
00:02:40,481 --> 00:02:41,976
crooked and bubbly.

82
00:02:42,156 --> 00:02:44,411
The fix is double-sided tape,

83
00:02:44,411 --> 00:02:47,367
or just three little pieces of regular

84
00:02:47,367 --> 00:02:48,379
tape, hidden.

85
00:02:48,559 --> 00:02:50,998
Fold the edge of the paper under,

86
00:02:50,998 --> 00:02:52,402
about half an inch,

87
00:02:52,402 --> 00:02:53,880
so it makes a clean,

88
00:02:53,880 --> 00:02:57,133
straight line, then tape it underneath where

89
00:02:57,133 --> 00:02:58,241
nobody can see.

90
00:02:58,591 --> 00:03:00,799
And fold that edge under,

91
00:03:00,799 --> 00:03:02,388
honey, every time.

92
00:03:02,568 --> 00:03:04,773
A raw cut edge always looks a

93
00:03:04,773 --> 00:03:07,282
little ragged, even if you cut it

94
00:03:07,282 --> 00:03:07,967
straight.

95
00:03:08,147 --> 00:03:10,184
A folded edge looks crisp,

96
00:03:10,184 --> 00:03:12,457
like a crease in a man's good

97
00:03:12,457 --> 00:03:13,162
trousers.

98
00:03:13,342 --> 00:03:16,034
Then run your thumbnail along every fold

99
00:03:16,034 --> 00:03:17,313
before you tape it.

100
00:03:17,493 --> 00:03:20,002
Sharp creases are the whole secret to

101
00:03:20,002 --> 00:03:22,240
a present that looks like it came

102
00:03:22,240 --> 00:03:23,054
from a shop.

103
00:03:23,403 --> 00:03:25,273
Mistake number three.

104
00:03:25,453 --> 00:03:27,693
Busy paper with a busy bow.

105
00:03:27,873 --> 00:03:30,679
Paper covered in Santas and snowmen and

106
00:03:30,679 --> 00:03:33,412
candy canes, with a shiny stick-on bow

107
00:03:33,412 --> 00:03:34,922
in a different color,

108
00:03:34,922 --> 00:03:37,080
and a curly ribbon in another.

109
00:03:37,260 --> 00:03:38,677
It's too much.

110
00:03:38,857 --> 00:03:41,074
Pick one thing to be the star.

111
00:03:41,254 --> 00:03:43,058
If the paper is busy,

112
00:03:43,058 --> 00:03:44,691
use a plain ribbon.

113
00:03:44,871 --> 00:03:46,646
If the paper is plain,

114
00:03:46,646 --> 00:03:49,228
then the ribbon and the trim can

115
00:03:49,228 --> 00:03:49,712
shine.

116
00:03:50,061 --> 00:03:52,964
And that's why plain paper looks richer,

117
00:03:52,964 --> 00:03:54,125
like I promised.

118
00:03:54,305 --> 00:03:56,057
Brown kraft paper,

119
00:03:56,057 --> 00:03:58,005
white butcher paper,

120
00:03:58,005 --> 00:03:59,855
or one solid color.

121
00:04:00,035 --> 00:04:02,237
It costs a third of the price

122
00:04:02,237 --> 00:04:03,831
of the printed rolls.

123
00:04:04,011 --> 00:04:05,775
Then add a nice ribbon,

124
00:04:05,775 --> 00:04:07,232
some real greenery,

125
00:04:07,232 --> 00:04:08,919
and a handwritten tag,

126
00:04:08,919 --> 00:04:11,527
and it looks like something from a

127
00:04:11,527 --> 00:04:12,370
fancy shop.

128
00:04:12,550 --> 00:04:15,161
The plain paper lets the pretty things

129
00:04:15,161 --> 00:04:15,847
stand out.

130
00:04:16,198 --> 00:04:18,113
Mistake number four.

131
00:04:18,293 --> 00:04:20,778
Those stick-on plastic bows.

132
00:04:20,958 --> 00:04:22,966
They come in a bag of fifty

133
00:04:22,966 --> 00:04:24,156
for two dollars,

134
00:04:24,156 --> 00:04:26,016
they fall off in the car,

135
00:04:26,016 --> 00:04:28,694
and they look exactly like what they

136
00:04:28,694 --> 00:04:28,992
are.

137
00:04:29,172 --> 00:04:30,519
Here's the fix.

138
00:04:30,699 --> 00:04:33,812
Real ribbon, tied around the box both

139
00:04:33,812 --> 00:04:35,410
ways, like a cross,

140
00:04:35,410 --> 00:04:37,514
with a proper bow on top.

141
00:04:37,694 --> 00:04:39,894
Use wired ribbon if you can,

142
00:04:39,894 --> 00:04:42,566
because it holds its shape and the

143
00:04:42,566 --> 00:04:44,845
loops stand up nice and full.

144
00:04:45,196 --> 00:04:46,887
If this is helping you,

145
00:04:46,887 --> 00:04:49,387
honey, tap the like button for me.

146
00:04:49,567 --> 00:04:52,133
It helps Grandma more than you know.

147
00:04:52,313 --> 00:04:54,400
Now here's how to tie a bow

148
00:04:54,400 --> 00:04:56,411
that lies flat and pretty.

149
00:04:56,591 --> 00:04:57,871
Make two loops,

150
00:04:57,871 --> 00:04:59,236
like bunny ears,

151
00:04:59,236 --> 00:05:00,943
and cross them over,

152
00:05:00,943 --> 00:05:02,223
tuck one under,

153
00:05:02,223 --> 00:05:03,418
pull it tight.

154
00:05:03,598 --> 00:05:06,021
Then pinch the loops and pull them

155
00:05:06,021 --> 00:05:08,160
out, so they're the same size.

156
00:05:08,340 --> 00:05:10,810
And cut the ends on a slant,

157
00:05:10,810 --> 00:05:11,692
or in a V,

158
00:05:11,692 --> 00:05:13,367
so they don't fray.

159
00:05:13,718 --> 00:05:16,122
And a cheap trick for ribbon,

160
00:05:16,122 --> 00:05:16,620
honey.

161
00:05:16,800 --> 00:05:20,344
Plain cotton string or baker's twine,

162
00:05:20,344 --> 00:05:21,685
red and white,

163
00:05:21,685 --> 00:05:23,696
costs almost nothing.

164
00:05:23,876 --> 00:05:26,358
Wrap it around three or four times

165
00:05:26,358 --> 00:05:27,963
and tie a simple knot.

166
00:05:28,143 --> 00:05:30,611
Or use strips of fabric torn from

167
00:05:30,611 --> 00:05:33,379
an old flannel shirt or a tablecloth.

168
00:05:33,559 --> 00:05:36,403
My mother used the leftover red calico

169
00:05:36,403 --> 00:05:39,097
from her kitchen curtains for years.

170
00:05:39,277 --> 00:05:41,750
People asked her where she bought it.

171
00:05:42,100 --> 00:05:45,065
So far, the right amount of paper,

172
00:05:45,065 --> 00:05:47,595
hidden tape and folded edges,

173
00:05:47,595 --> 00:05:50,735
one star, and real ribbon instead of

174
00:05:50,735 --> 00:05:51,956
stick-on bows.

175
00:05:52,136 --> 00:05:54,238
Now mistake number five.

176
00:05:54,418 --> 00:05:55,614
A sticker tag,

177
00:05:55,614 --> 00:05:58,606
or worse, writing on the paper with

178
00:05:58,606 --> 00:05:59,375
a marker.

179
00:05:59,555 --> 00:06:02,806
A real tag makes all the difference.

180
00:06:02,986 --> 00:06:05,419
Cut them from old Christmas cards,

181
00:06:05,419 --> 00:06:06,707
the front picture,

182
00:06:06,707 --> 00:06:09,139
with a hole punched in the corner.

183
00:06:09,319 --> 00:06:11,154
Tie it on with the ribbon.

184
00:06:11,503 --> 00:06:13,188
And write it by hand,

185
00:06:13,188 --> 00:06:15,834
sweetheart, in your best writing.

186
00:06:16,014 --> 00:06:17,883
Not just to and from.

187
00:06:18,063 --> 00:06:19,863
Add one little line.

188
00:06:20,043 --> 00:06:21,831
For my favorite baker.

189
00:06:22,011 --> 00:06:24,240
For the best brother in Ohio.

190
00:06:24,420 --> 00:06:27,151
My brother Danny still has every tag

191
00:06:27,151 --> 00:06:28,440
I ever wrote him,

192
00:06:28,440 --> 00:06:29,578
in a cigar box,

193
00:06:29,578 --> 00:06:31,096
sixty years of them.

194
00:06:31,276 --> 00:06:33,363
He showed me last Christmas,

195
00:06:33,363 --> 00:06:35,004
and I had to go sit in

196
00:06:35,004 --> 00:06:36,570
the car for a minute.

197
00:06:36,920 --> 00:06:38,661
Mistake number six.

198
00:06:38,841 --> 00:06:41,465
Trying to wrap odd shapes in paper.

199
00:06:41,645 --> 00:06:43,329
A bottle, a ball,

200
00:06:43,329 --> 00:06:44,815
a stuffed bear,

201
00:06:44,815 --> 00:06:46,103
a frying pan.

202
00:06:46,283 --> 00:06:48,652
You end up with a crumpled mess.

203
00:06:48,832 --> 00:06:50,494
The fix is a box,

204
00:06:50,494 --> 00:06:51,374
or a bag.

205
00:06:51,554 --> 00:06:54,203
Put odd things in any old box

206
00:06:54,203 --> 00:06:55,755
from the kitchen,

207
00:06:55,755 --> 00:06:59,135
a shoebox, a cereal box turned inside

208
00:06:59,135 --> 00:07:01,144
out, and wrap the box.

209
00:07:01,324 --> 00:07:03,680
Nobody can guess what's inside,

210
00:07:03,680 --> 00:07:05,504
and that's half the fun.

211
00:07:05,854 --> 00:07:07,898
And for really big things,

212
00:07:07,898 --> 00:07:09,078
like a bicycle,

213
00:07:09,078 --> 00:07:10,730
don't wrap it at all.

214
00:07:10,910 --> 00:07:12,570
Put a big bow on it and

215
00:07:12,570 --> 00:07:14,663
hide it behind a sheet in the

216
00:07:14,663 --> 00:07:16,900
corner until Christmas morning.

217
00:07:17,080 --> 00:07:19,079
Or wrap a tiny box with a

218
00:07:19,079 --> 00:07:21,797
clue inside, and a treasure map to

219
00:07:21,797 --> 00:07:22,676
the garage.

220
00:07:22,856 --> 00:07:25,539
My father did that for Danny's first

221
00:07:25,539 --> 00:07:27,849
bicycle in nineteen fifty-five.

222
00:07:28,029 --> 00:07:29,664
Danny cried so hard,

223
00:07:29,664 --> 00:07:31,953
he couldn't find the garage.

224
00:07:32,302 --> 00:07:34,484
And now mistake number seven,

225
00:07:34,484 --> 00:07:36,064
the trick I promised.

226
00:07:36,244 --> 00:07:38,815
A present with nothing tucked in the

227
00:07:38,815 --> 00:07:39,100
bow.

228
00:07:39,280 --> 00:07:41,869
It's finished in ten seconds.

229
00:07:42,049 --> 00:07:44,662
Slide a little sprig of pine or

230
00:07:44,662 --> 00:07:46,684
cedar, a cinnamon stick,

231
00:07:46,684 --> 00:07:47,780
a candy cane,

232
00:07:47,780 --> 00:07:49,549
a dried orange slice,

233
00:07:49,549 --> 00:07:52,246
or a sprig of rosemary under the

234
00:07:52,246 --> 00:07:54,437
ribbon, right in the knot.

235
00:07:54,617 --> 00:07:56,974
That's the thing people save.

236
00:07:57,154 --> 00:07:58,840
They take it off and put it

237
00:07:58,840 --> 00:07:59,963
on the windowsill.

238
00:08:00,314 --> 00:08:03,147
My mother tucked a little pinecone in

239
00:08:03,147 --> 00:08:05,980
every bow, painted white at the tips,

240
00:08:05,980 --> 00:08:06,745
like snow.

241
00:08:06,925 --> 00:08:09,287
She'd collect them in October with the

242
00:08:09,287 --> 00:08:12,021
grandchildren, and paint them at the kitchen

243
00:08:12,021 --> 00:08:12,394
table.

244
00:08:12,574 --> 00:08:14,813
Years later, I found a box in

245
00:08:14,813 --> 00:08:17,746
her closet with dozens of those little

246
00:08:17,746 --> 00:08:18,518
pinecones.

247
00:08:18,698 --> 00:08:21,206
People had given them back to her,

248
00:08:21,206 --> 00:08:24,156
saying, Dorothy, these are too pretty to

249
00:08:24,156 --> 00:08:24,967
throw away.

250
00:08:25,318 --> 00:08:27,506
And if you have little ones at

251
00:08:27,506 --> 00:08:29,914
home, honey, give them a job like

252
00:08:29,914 --> 00:08:31,227
my mother gave me.

253
00:08:31,407 --> 00:08:34,173
Let them stamp the plain paper with

254
00:08:34,173 --> 00:08:36,782
a cut potato dipped in red paint,

255
00:08:36,782 --> 00:08:37,967
stars or trees,

256
00:08:37,967 --> 00:08:39,232
before you wrap.

257
00:08:39,412 --> 00:08:41,212
Let it dry overnight.

258
00:08:41,392 --> 00:08:43,954
It's the prettiest paper in the world,

259
00:08:43,954 --> 00:08:46,651
and the grandparents will never throw it

260
00:08:46,651 --> 00:08:46,988
away.

261
00:08:47,168 --> 00:08:51,029
My granddaughter's potato paper from nineteen ninety-six

262
00:08:51,029 --> 00:08:52,891
is still in my cedar chest.

263
00:08:53,241 --> 00:08:55,593
And a few last tips for saving

264
00:08:55,593 --> 00:08:56,063
money.

265
00:08:56,243 --> 00:08:59,152
Buy ribbon and paper the day after

266
00:08:59,152 --> 00:09:01,291
Christmas, at half price,

267
00:09:01,291 --> 00:09:02,489
for next year.

268
00:09:02,669 --> 00:09:04,550
Save the boxes you get.

269
00:09:04,730 --> 00:09:07,610
Gift bags can be used ten times,

270
00:09:07,610 --> 00:09:10,041
so ask for them back with a

271
00:09:10,041 --> 00:09:10,581
smile.

272
00:09:10,761 --> 00:09:13,258
Fold the good tissue paper flat and

273
00:09:13,258 --> 00:09:14,685
keep it in a drawer.

274
00:09:14,865 --> 00:09:17,288
My mother's wrapping cost her about a

275
00:09:17,288 --> 00:09:18,205
dollar a year,

276
00:09:18,205 --> 00:09:20,235
and it was the prettiest on the

277
00:09:20,235 --> 00:09:20,694
street.

278
00:09:21,043 --> 00:09:23,557
And set up a wrapping station like

279
00:09:23,557 --> 00:09:24,666
she did, honey.

280
00:09:24,846 --> 00:09:27,118
Everything in one basket,

281
00:09:27,118 --> 00:09:29,209
scissors, tape, ribbon,

282
00:09:29,209 --> 00:09:30,300
tags, a pen,

283
00:09:30,300 --> 00:09:32,845
and a little bag for scraps.

284
00:09:33,025 --> 00:09:35,037
Put on some Christmas music,

285
00:09:35,037 --> 00:09:36,474
make a cup of cocoa,

286
00:09:36,474 --> 00:09:38,630
and wrap a few every night the

287
00:09:38,630 --> 00:09:39,492
week before.

288
00:09:39,672 --> 00:09:42,832
Wrapping twenty presents on Christmas Eve at

289
00:09:42,832 --> 00:09:45,633
midnight is how presents end up looking

290
00:09:45,633 --> 00:09:46,998
like lumpy pillows.

291
00:09:47,347 --> 00:09:49,182
So one more time.

292
00:09:49,362 --> 00:09:50,942
Cut the paper to fit,

293
00:09:50,942 --> 00:09:52,973
with two inches of overlap.

294
00:09:53,153 --> 00:09:55,421
Fold the edges under and hide the

295
00:09:55,421 --> 00:09:55,765
tape.

296
00:09:55,945 --> 00:09:58,998
Run your thumbnail along every crease.

297
00:09:59,178 --> 00:10:01,349
Plain paper with pretty ribbon,

298
00:10:01,349 --> 00:10:03,590
or busy paper with plain ribbon.

299
00:10:03,770 --> 00:10:06,974
Real ribbon, tied both ways.

300
00:10:07,154 --> 00:10:08,710
A handwritten tag.

301
00:10:08,890 --> 00:10:10,968
Odd shapes in a box.

302
00:10:11,148 --> 00:10:13,285
And something tucked in the bow.

303
00:10:13,465 --> 00:10:16,100
That's how my mother wrapped Christmas.

304
00:10:16,449 --> 00:10:18,503
Tell me in the comments,

305
00:10:18,503 --> 00:10:19,445
sweetheart.

306
00:10:19,625 --> 00:10:21,815
Are you a careful wrapper,

307
00:10:21,815 --> 00:10:23,584
or a gift bag person?

308
00:10:23,764 --> 00:10:26,051
No judging, I promise.

309
00:10:26,231 --> 00:10:29,450
The whole playlist of Grandma's Christmas tips

310
00:10:29,450 --> 00:10:31,549
is right there on your screen,

311
00:10:31,549 --> 00:10:33,509
and I'll see you in the next

312
00:10:33,509 --> 00:10:33,928
video.

313
00:10:34,108 --> 00:10:35,769
Good night, honey.

314
00:10:35,949 --> 00:10:37,423
Merry Christmas.
